WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.395
"Is One Egg a Day Too Much?"

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.320
The NIH-AARP study,

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:11.480
sponsored by the National
Institutes of Health

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.480
and the American Association
of Retired Persons,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.680
is the largest forward-looking,
in-depth study

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.080
on diet and health in history.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.840
Based on its six million
person-years of observation,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.680
replacing just three percent
of daily caloric intake

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.120
of animal protein
with plant protein

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.200
was associated
with a 10 percent decreased risk

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.840
of premature death.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.480
Just a three percent swap!

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.240
Of all the animal
protein sources,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:38.240
eggs were found
to be the worst.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.760
Swapping in three percent
of plant protein for egg protein

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.400
was associated
with twice the benefit,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.240
more than 20 percent
lower mortality,

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.920
having scrambled tofu instead.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.560
In fact, each half an egg a day

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.640
was associated
with a seven percent increase

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.320
in all-cause mortality.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.480
Just half an egg?

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.200
That's consistent with six
other U.S. cohort studies

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.200
that collectively found

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.720
that each additional
half an egg consumed per day

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.760
was significantly associated
with a higher risk

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.840
of getting
cardiovascular disease

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.400
and dying prematurely
from all causes put together.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.560
And this higher risk of death
persisted

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.640
even after taking
other lifestyle behaviors

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.000
into account, including
overall dietary quality.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.280
In other words,
it doesn't appear to just be

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.680
because people eating more eggs

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.000
were also eating more bacon,
for example.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.840
A meta-analysis
of all such studies

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.440
confirmed that
greater egg consumption

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.920
confers higher risks of death
from all causes.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:31.960
Another meta-analysis

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.360
including other types
of studies

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.680
involving nearly
12 million participants

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:39.440
echoed that it may be prudent
to avoid high egg consumption.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.360
But what does "high" mean?

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.200
What's the safe amount?

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.920
It's not quite clear.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.400
One meta-analysis suggests
there's little evidence

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.240
for elevated risks
below half an egg a day,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.040
whereas another found
that adding

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.360
even one egg a week
to your diet

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.160
might increase your risk
of dying from cancer.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.960
If the data on detriment
are so clear,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.320
based on studies
involving millions of people,

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.200
why is there still controversy?

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.720
Because the egg industry
manufactures that controversy.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.480
Just like the sugar industry
does with sugar.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.120
Dr. Barnard's written about it.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.800
I've written about it.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.680
As noted by the director
of the Stroke Prevention

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.800
and Atherosclerosis
Research Centre,

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.280
"After being convicted
for false advertising

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.240
for suggesting
that eggs were safe,

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.880
the egg industry has spent
hundreds of millions of dollars

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.440
trying to convince the public,
physicians, and policy makers

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.840
that dietary cholesterol
and eggs are harmless."

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.280
Yet a meta-analysis

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:33.520
of more than 50
randomized controlled trials

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.000
found that egg consumption

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.560
significantly increases
LDL cholesterol.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.080
Online, some bloggers parrot

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.840
the egg industry's
talking points

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.480
that the 2015 U.S.
dietary guidelines

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.440
removed the dietary
cholesterol limit

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.960
to consume
less than 300 milligrams

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.200
of dietary cholesterol a day,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.560
whereas if anyone bothered
to actually read

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.200
the actual guidelines,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.280
they'd see that the guidelines
actually strengthen

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.720
the recommendation
and tell Americans

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.720
to "eat as little dietary
cholesterol as possible,"

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.440
as recommended
by the Institute of Medicine,

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.920
the most prestigious
medical body

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.560
in the United States.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.200
This advice was reiterated

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.240
in the most recent
dietary guidelines:

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.760
"The National Academies
recommends

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.280
that dietary
cholesterol consumption

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.360
be as low as possible,"

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.120
using the rationale
that any intake above zero

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.720
increases LDL cholesterol
concentration in the blood

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.120
and, therefore,
increases the risk

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.674
of the number one killer
of men and women.

